(Thermodynamics basics - page 1) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
0 -30 Td
18 TL
(Zeroth law of thermodynamics. If two systems are each in thermal equilibrium) '
(with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. Two) '
(systems in thermal equilibrium have the same temperature. Temperature is what a) '
(thermometer measures. Heat is energy that flows from a hotter body to a colder) '
(one.) '
ET
endstream
endobj
4 0 obj
<< /Length 468 >>
stream
BT
/F2 16 Tf
50 790 Td
(Thermodynamics basics - page 2) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
0 -30 Td
18 TL
(First law of thermodynamics. Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only) '
(transferred or converted. The change in internal energy equals the heat added) '
(minus the work done by the system. An isolated system exchanges neither matter) '
(nor energy with its surroundings. A closed system exchanges energy but not) '
(matter. An open system exchanges both matter and energy.) '
ET
endstream
endobj
5 0 obj
<< /Length 576 >>
stream
BT
/F2 16 Tf
50 790 Td
(Thermodynamics basics - page 3) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
0 -30 Td
18 TL
(Second law of thermodynamics. The entropy of an isolated system never decreases.) '
(Heat flows spontaneously from hot to cold, never the other way without work. A) '
(reversible process is an idealisation in which the entropy stays constant. Third) '
(law of thermodynamics. As temperature approaches absolute zero, the entropy of a) '
(perfect crystal approaches zero. Absolute zero is 0 kelvin, or minus 273.15) '
(degrees Celsius, and cannot be reached in a finite number of steps.) '
ET
endstream
endobj
6 0 obj
<< /Length 360 >>
stream
BT
/F2 16 Tf
50 790 Td
(Thermodynamics basics - page 4) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
0 -30 Td
18 TL
(Entropy. Entropy is a measure of the disorder of a system, or the number of) '
(microscopic arrangements that match its overall state. Ice melting increases) '
(entropy. A gas expanding into a vacuum also increases entropy. Entropy is) '
(measured in joules per kelvin.) '
ET
endstream
endobj
7 0 obj
<< /Length 461 >>
stream
BT
/F2 16 Tf
50 790 Td
(Thermodynamics basics - page 5) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
0 -30 Td
18 TL
(Heat engines. A heat engine turns heat into work by moving heat from a hot) '
(reservoir to a cold reservoir. Car engines and power stations are heat engines.) '
(Efficiency is the work done divided by the heat taken from the hot reservoir.) '
(Some heat must be rejected to the cold reservoir, as required by the second law,) '
(so no heat engine can be 100% efficient.) '
ET
endstream
endobj
8 0 obj
<< /Length 458 >>
stream
BT
/F2 16 Tf
50 790 Td
(Thermodynamics basics - page 6) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
0 -30 Td
18 TL
(Carnot efficiency. Carnot efficiency is the maximum efficiency of a heat engine) '
(between two reservoirs: 1 - Tc/Th, where Tc is the cold temperature and Th is) '
(the hot temperature. All temperatures must be in kelvin. Real engines always) '
(fall short of this limit. A refrigerator is a heat engine run backwards: it uses) '
(work to move heat from cold to hot.) '
ET
endstream
endobj
